```java
public interface ValidationRule<T> {
    List<String> check(T target);
}

public class CompositeValidator<T> implements ValidationRule<T> {
    private final List<ValidationRule<T>> rules;

    public List<String> check(T target) {
        List<String> errors = new ArrayList<>();
        for (ValidationRule<T> rule : rules) errors.addAll(rule.check(target));
        return errors;
    }
}
```

Returning a list of messages — or better, a list of structured error objects, per [designing errors](designing-errors) — rather than throwing on the first violation is the whole design: a caller gets every problem in one call, which is what a form UI actually needs.

```java
public class DateRangeRule implements ValidationRule<Booking> {
    public List<String> check(Booking b) {
        if (b.startDate().isAfter(b.endDate())) {
            return List.of("startDate must be before endDate");
        }
        return List.of();
    }
}
```

This rule couldn't exist if `Rule<T>` only received one field's value — it needs both `startDate` and `endDate` from the same `Booking` at once. Designing the interface around the whole target object, not a single field, is what makes this rule possible without a special case.
